===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[lay out]] on the grass; to [[knock down]] (an opponent etc.).
#: {{syn|en|flatten|floor|lay low|lay out|knock down|knock out|knock over|strike down}}
#* {{RQ:Dickens Dombey and Son|passage=The Chicken himself attributed this punishment to his having had the misfortune to get into Chancery early in the proceedings, when he was severely fibbed by the Larkey one, and heavily '''grassed'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1893|author=Arthur Conan Doyle|title=The Naval Treaty|publisher=Norton|year_published=2005|page=709|passage=He flew at me with his knife, and I had to '''grass''' him twice, and got a cut over the knuckles, before I had the upper hand of him.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|or|intransitive|slang}} To act as a grass or [[informer]], to [[betray]]; to report on (criminals etc) to the authorities.
#: {{syn|en|Thesaurus:rat out}}
#* {{RQ:William Burroughs Naked Lunch|page=2|passage="'''Grassed''' on me he did," I said morosely. (Note: Grass is English thief slang for inform.){{sic}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2004|author=David Nobbs|title=Sex and Other Changes|url=https://books.google.com.hk/books?id=ic-Chb9y6W8C&pg=PA95|page=95|passage="I'm dressed as a woman, but I am still technically a man. I believe that to comply with the law of the land I ought to continue to use the [[gents'|Gents']], but in order not to look out place I intend to use the [[ladies'|Ladies']] from now on. I trust none of you will [[grass]] on me..."}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To cover with grass or with [[turf]].
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[feed]] with grass.
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To expose, as [[flax]], on the grass for [[bleach]]ing, etc.
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To bring to the grass or ground; to [[land]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1903|author=w:John Buchan|title=The African Colony
|passage=Let him hook and land a tigerfish of 20 lb., at the imminent risk of capsizing and joining the company of the engaging crocodiles, or, when he has '''grassed''' the fish, of having a finger bitten off by his iron teeth {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2011|author=Deeanne Gist|title=Love on the Line|page=138|passage=In typical Necker style, the farmer walked to the line and mounted his gun without any shilly-shally. If he '''grassed''' the bird, he and Faurote would go into a shootout. If he missed, Faurote would win.}}

